<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silla–Tang War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/670">670</a>–676</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Silla–Tang War was fought from 670 to 676 between the Korean kingdom of Silla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, its former ally against Baekje and Goguryeo. Silla resisted Tang plans to rule the conquered lands and supported Goguryeo rebels. After Silla's victories at Maeso Fortress in 675 and Gibeolpo in 676, the Tang withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, and Silla controlled the peninsula south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silla-tang-war.jpg" alt="Silla–Tang War" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EB%82%98%EB%8B%B9%EC%A0%84%EC%9F%81%EA%B3%BC_%EB%B3%B4%EB%8D%95%EA%B5%AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Goguryeo–Tang War ended in 668, the lands of Baekje and Goguryeo were occupied by the Silla–Tang alliance, and many of their people were forcibly resettled by the Tang. Emperor Gaozong of Tang created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate-general-to-pacify-the-east">Protectorate General to Pacify the East</a>, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, and divided Goguryeo's territory into 9 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, 42 prefectures and 100 counties. Silla resented this: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Emperor Taizong of Tang</a> had promised it Baekje and the lands south of Pyongyang in return for its help, but Gaozong reneged on the promise.</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munmu-of-silla">Munmu of Silla</a> assembled an army that included the remnants of the Baekje and Goguryeo forces, aiming to drive the Tang from the peninsula. Revolts in the former Goguryeo lands, the first in 669, aided his cause.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 671 Silla drove the Tang out of most of the former Baekje territory. In February 675 the Tang defeated Silla at Chiljung Fortress in Gyeonggi; Munmu sent a tributary mission with apologies, and Gaozong, who accepted them, withdrew troops to meet the Tibetan threat in the west. Silla then renewed its advance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-rengui">Xue Rengui</a>, who crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> to supply the army of Li Jinxing, was defeated at Cheonseong with the loss of 1,400 men, 1,000 horses and 40 warships.</p><p>According to the Samguk Sagi, Li Jinxing led 200,000 soldiers at Maeso Fortress in September 675 and was defeated, and 30,080 war horses were captured. Chinese and Korean sources each tend to omit or play down the battles their side lost, and the Zizhi Tongjian does not mention Maeso. In November 676 a Silla victory at Gibeolpo, a naval battle of which little more is known, ended the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Tang moved the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a>, which ended their designs on Silla. The frontier between Tang and Unified Silla ran from the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a> to the East Korea Bay; the lands to the north stayed under Tang rule and later mostly passed to Balhae. Relations were restored in the early 8th century under King Seongdeok of Silla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuanzong-of-tang">Emperor Xuanzong of Tang</a>, and in 736 the Tang formally recognised Silla's rule south of the Taedong River.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Silla–Tang War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
